!! The @nexus lifecycle ~ born by act, lives by traffic, quiet by absence

''THE MYTH HOLDS THE MODEL'' (operator, 2026-08-07). A Nexus names several vessels syncing across a ''ley-line
network'' — so ''no network traffic, no live Nexus''. Liveness reads off the lines themselves; nothing
declares it, and nobody grants it.

''WHICH ANSWERS DISSOLUTION WITHOUT AN ADMIN CAPABILITY.'' A Nexus is never dissolved — ''it goes quiet''. That
rhymes with every neighbouring ruling rather than inventing a new shape: a cabal-realm ''DISSOLVES BY COOLING''
and never by an act; a standing lift ''EXPIRES'' and no party holds a lowering act; //nobody dissolves a Cabal
by preventing its meetings//. ''The correct admin capability here reads as NONE.''

\procedure ~Stage(~Type:"" ~Params:"") ~Stage <<~Type>> <<~holds `[<~Params>]`>>

<<~Stage Birth "verb/nexus-seal-seat admin/none ~ the operator's own personas declare and stand" >>
<<~Stage Advance "verb/nexus-seal-rotate admin/none ~ pre-committed, quorum-bound" >>
<<~Stage Succession "verb/nexus-seal-rotate admin/none ~ the roster becomes exactly what STANDS" >>
<<~Stage Liveness "verb/none admin/none ~ ley-line traffic; nothing to build" >>
<<~Stage Quiet "verb/none admin/none ~ the absence of traffic, and that IS the point" >>

''SUCCESSION RIDES ROTATE, and needed no new verb.'' A kahu steps down by ''no longer standing''; a new one
takes a chair by declaring a Handle and standing. A rotation then seats the roster that stands — and the
''reveal must still match the prior epoch's pre-commitment'', so a roster change lands only where the operator
pre-committed that exact successor key-set an epoch earlier, in the open, under the quorum standing then.
''Nobody adds or drops a kahu quietly'', and no hand holds a capability to do it.

''You cannot change who holds the quorum without changing what the quorum is'' — which is why the succession
door was already there wearing another name.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #bag-home >>

!! Where a bag rests ~ repository ⊥ hearth ⊥ ley

A bag already declares ''who may read it'' (`CapTier`) and ''whether it stands loaded'' (residency
temperature). Neither answers ''where its bytes rest'', and one pair proves the axis stands apart: the canon
memes and the Nexus seal read as equally PUBLIC and want ''opposite homes''.

\procedure ~Home(~Type:"" ~Params:"") ~Home <<~Type>> <<~holds `[<~Params>]`>>

<<~Home Repository "rests/a-configured-source-control-repo clone-carries/yes ~ canon, memes, seeds — the history IS the point" >>
<<~Home Hearth "rests/per-operator-state clone-carries/no ~ identity · the seal · the follow-graph · the persona names" >>
<<~Home Ley "rests/nowhere-durable clone-carries/nothing ~ it lives while the mesh carries it" >>

''FAIL-CLOSED TO HEARTH, and the asymmetry carries the reason.'' Only `hearth` can be wrong quietly — a bag
that should have ridden the repository merely fails to be shared, and an operator notices. The other two fail
where it hurts: a mis-defaulted `repository` writes a private thing into a history somebody may already have
pushed, and a mis-defaulted `ley` gives a durable thing no durable home. ''The default takes the recoverable
failure.''

''WHAT ENFORCES IT, and the bypass, named.'' `resolveBagHomeDir` holds the ONLY mapping from a declared home
to a directory. A caller that ''joins its own path'' bypasses it entirely and nothing there can stop it —
which is precisely how the seal came to sit inside a repository, off a call site reading `LAR_BAGS ?? join(root,
"bags")`. So this stands as ''a mechanism with a named bypass rather than a guarantee'', and the discipline
runs: a new bag asks for its directory, and a hand-rolled `join(…bags…)` invites the question why.
''`repository` refuses outright where no repo stands configured'' — guessing a tree is the failure the axis
exists to prevent.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #preflight >>

!! ⓪′ Preflight ~ prove the toolchain answers BEFORE anything irreversible

''Every movement below assumes a binary that loads, and the rite never checked.'' A vessel cleared by a
toolchain that cannot then found leaves an operator holding neither — the clear runs irreversible and the
founding runs unavailable, and nothing in the sequence catches it in between.

<<~ confidence Canon 17/20 >> ''Measured 2026-08-04'': the `lares` entry point died at import on a symbol
absent from every source and present only in a built artifact — a stale `dist` against a renamed export.
The error named an internal symbol, not a build, and an operator reading it mid-rite has no way to reach
`rebuild` from what it says. ''A full rebuild cured it.''

```
pnpm -r build                                       # the dist must match the source it imports from
lares help                                          # the binary loads and answers — the ONLY gate this proves
pnpm outdated -r                                    # bump BEFORE, never after (see below)
pnpm exec playwright install chromium               # ONLY after a playwright bump — the binaries pin to it
```

''A playwright bump strands its own browsers.'' The version pins a chromium build, so the browser suite dies
at launch with //Executable doesn't exist// until the matching binary lands. Measured 2026-08-09; the first
run after the install threw one cold-start flake and two re-runs came back clean, so ''a single browser red
immediately after an install earns a re-run before it earns a diagnosis''.

<<~ confidence Canon 17/20 >> ''Walked 2026-08-09.'' The whole tree came current, built green, and stood
1682 specs + 3318 tests with zero failures — so this movement now reports a walk rather than a plan. The
vendored `tiddlywiki` keeps its ''own'' eslint generation: its majors belong to that submodule, and bumping
them from here moves the engine blob that rides inside every signed delegation preimage.

''BUMP THE DEPS BEFORE, NEVER AFTER — and the reason reads narrower than the fear.'' A runtime bump
(automerge · noble · ws · vite) moves ''NO epoch binding'': the engine blob is vendored TW5 and the built
plugin carries zero automerge references, both measured. It can skew the ''on-disk serde'' alone, which
`lares rebuild` cures identity-safe. So bumping first reads as hygiene rather than a cliff.

''The surfaces that WOULD be a cliff do not move on npm at all'': `hearthTrueName` (the engine CID, signed
into every device-delegation edge) and the `pluginsCid` (built from this repo's own source). Those move by
an ACT — a TW5 submodule bump, or a commit — never by an upgrade.
<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/epoch-binding-surfaces >>

''Run this at the head of BOTH branches.'' A rehearsal that opens on a stale toolchain measures the
toolchain and reports nothing about the rite.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #clear >>

!! ① Clear ~ pare to void (⚠ irreversible — the KEEPER branch)

''Branch check first.'' A REHEARSAL never reaches this movement — its clear rides `compose down -v`
(#rehearse) and it keeps nothing. What follows governs the ''KEEPER'' founding alone. ''⓪′ Preflight
stands as a precondition'', never a courtesy — it names the one failure this movement cannot undo.

A fresh-from-void founding discards the prior identity. ''Back the old identity up FIRST'' — the
vault seals nothing until movement ④, so a raw copy carries the only net before the nuke:

```
lares status                                        # confirm the identity-home path
cp -r ~/.local/state/lares/identity ~/founding-backup-PRIOR   # the only pre-nuke net
lares hooks pause                                   # still the capture/ingest hooks during the pave
```

Then clear. ''⚠ The next line destroys the prior persona-roots + the keyhive archive — it does not
undo.'' A vessel that carries nothing worth keeping (the bootstrap case) crosses it freely:

```
rm -rf ~/.local/state/lares/identity                # ⚠ IRREVERSIBLE: the fresh-from-void cut
rm -rf ~/.local/state/lares/nexus                   # ⚠ IRREVERSIBLE: the prior SEAL (see below)
lares reset --force                                 # pave the vessel store (the identity home now stands empty)
```

''TWO SOVEREIGN THINGS SURVIVE A RESET, and BOTH want cutting here.'' `reset --force` pares the vessel store
and preserves the state home — that boundary IS the //share substrate, not sovereignty// law, and the sovereign
root sits behind it on purpose. The Nexus SEAL sits behind it too, in its own home beside the identity
(`<state>/nexus/founding-roster.mem`), so a re-founding cuts both or strands itself: the seat ''FAILS CLOSED''
against a lineage already past genesis (//advance it with rotate, never re-seat//), and the operator holds a
fresh vessel that cannot seal.

''WHY THE SEAL HOMES THERE and not in the corpus.'' It reads as a FILE by ruling — an operator backs it up,
hands it to a peer, reads it with their own eyes — and a file sited in the bags tree ''inherited that tree's
home'', which on a development install sits inside the repository. A founding seal could have landed in a git
history nobody meant to write it into. Per-operator state cures it, and says something true besides: ''a clone
of the corpus carries NO Nexus'', because a Nexus belongs to the operators who founded it rather than to
whoever copied the code.

''A first founding crosses this freely'' — no seal stands, so the line removes nothing.

!!! ⚠ WHAT THE CLEAR MUST NOT TOUCH ~ the library survives every founding

''DO NOT `rm -rf ~/.local/state/lares`.'' Two lines above point INSIDE that directory, which makes the
whole-directory gesture read as the natural next reach — and it would destroy the one thing on the vessel
that regenerates from ''nothing''.

The state home carries ''four kinds'' and only two of them clear:

|! kind |! path |! the clear |
| the sovereign root | `<state>/identity` | ''CUT'' — the fresh-from-void act |
| the Nexus seal | `<state>/nexus` | ''CUT'' — a prior lineage strands a re-founding |
| the ACQUIRED library | `<state>/library` | ''KEEP'' — books, corpora, anything poured over. It rebuilds from NOTHING |
| the repo registry | `<state>/repos.json` | ''KEEP'' — ids the bags name; re-registering costs an act |

''The library holds what no rite can re-make.'' The runtime CAS may be pared because its blobs rebuild from
the bags carriers; an acquired body has no such path — the corpus left the repository precisely so a shelf
could grow without the history growing with it, and the shelf is now the only copy. Verify it after the
founding rather than trusting the absence of an error:

```
lares library verify                                # every body re-digests to the directory holding it
lares library list                                  # the collections, and what each holds
```

<<~ confidence Canon 18/20 >> ''A `reset` never reaches it'' — the tier homes under the STATE home for exactly
this reason (`<data>/vessel` is what the substrate verbs reforge). The danger the rite must name reads as the
''operator's own hand'', never the machine's.


<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #found >>

!! ② Found ~ stand the device + the three kahu

`wake --install` stands the device/vessel identity + the daemon + the founder persona-root h0
(h0's root signs the founding bind that peers pin — it MUST pre-exist). The three kahu then stand
as ''three symmetric commands'' — and each command performs ''THREE ACTS, never one'' (persona-policy,
2026-08-06). Stand h0 too; an unnamed founder strands itself out of its own charter:

```
lares wake --install                                # stands the VESSEL — and nothing else (#sidecar)
lares persona new 0 --name '<private label>' --handle 'Guru Joshua Fontany' --seat
lares persona new 1 --name '<private label>' --handle 'Telarus, KSC'        --seat
lares persona new 2 --name '<private label>' --handle 'The Lindwyrm'        --seat
lares persona list                                  # h0/h1/h2 stand: label -> declared Handle, each standing for a chair
```

''THE THREE ACTS, and why the seal reads only one of them.''

* `--name` sets the ''PRIVATE label'' — what the operator calls that compartment. It rides the human's
  own fleet (@persona) and ''never publicly federates''.
* `--handle` declares the ''PUBLIC Handle'' the persona answers to. A ''local intent'', not a publish:
  only a publicly announced Handle binds a PersonaGroup to a public glamour.
* `--seat` stands the persona for a ''Kahu chair on THIS node''. It stays local — a chair names a seat
  on a particular node, so it never rides the fleet.

''THE ROSTER FORMS FROM WHAT STOOD.'' No list of founding names ships in the build: `nexus seal seat` reads
the personas that ''declared a Handle AND stood for a chair'' and writes exactly those as the roster. A
scaffold carrying names would make the ''SOURCE'' decide who the founding kahu are, leaving the operator to
confirm a legitimacy call somebody else made — and legitimacy belongs where the acts happen. A persona that
declares and never stands ''takes no chair at all'', rather than an empty one somebody wrote down for it.

''THE THRESHOLD comes from the roster, or from the operator.'' Absent `--threshold`, the seat derives
''MAJORITY'' over what stood (3 chairs → 2). Majority reads as the weakest defensible default: it refuses a
single hand without handing any one kahu a veto. A `--threshold` past the roster ''REFUSES'' — it would seat
a rule no quorum could ever reach — and a seat with ''nobody standing'' refuses and writes nothing.

''THE SEAL JOINS ON THE DECLARED HANDLE, NEVER ON THE LABEL.'' Matching the private label would ''weld
the two registers'': an operator could then seat only under the same string they call the compartment at
home, and every private label would become a public commitment by construction. The two MAY read
word-for-word identical — the operator's `Telarus, KSC` names a compartment privately AND stands as a
chair name — and they stay ''two acts''. ''The declaring act makes a name public, never the characters.''

''Neither flag implies the other.'' A persona MAY declare a Handle and stand for no chair (the ordinary
case, and the shape every later persona takes). A `--seat` carrying no Handle ''answers to no chair'',
so `persona new` refuses it rather than seating a nameless claim, and a chair whose declared Handle
nobody stood for stays ''UNSEATED'' — the seal reports the empty chair rather than reading a declaration
as consent to sit in it.

''THIS MOVEMENT WRITES THE SELF-STOOD PATH, and a second path stands beside it'' (True Name Model,
2026-07-25). `FoundingBinding` names two modes as a union rather than inferring one from an optional
field:

* ''SELF-STOOD'' — the vessel carries a seed that SIGNS: it mints its own persona root and signs its
  own device-delegation, seating a fresh persona-KEL inception. The sequence above. A human's own
  hearth or leaf, present and binding their own device.
* ''CONTRACTED'' — the vessel mints ''NO root''. A trusted operator, already contracted with the
  kahu, signs the edge on THEIR vessel; this one carries only the ''EDGE'', the pinned identifier
  prefix, and that operator's KEL chain (carried, never fetched — a vessel holding no human key also
  holds no way to go ask for one). ''It seats no inception'': the contracting operator's identifier
  already stands, and minting a second would fork the very continuity the pin exists to hold.

''BIND BY EDGE, NEVER BY SEED.'' A key COPIED between vessels //is// the collector — presented to
every verifier it meets, it links every self beneath any policy written above it.

''⚠ ONE WORD, TWO SENSES — hold them apart.'' This rite uses ''contracted'' in both meanings and they
never coincide: a ''CONTRACTED FOUNDING'' (this movement) names HOW a vessel came to stand; a
''CARRIAGE-CONTRACTED OPERATOR'' (#grow-the-nexus) names an operator who agreed to CARRY for a Nexus.
A self-stood hearth may be carriage-contracted; a contracted-founding Herm carries without ever
holding a civic identity. ''Founding-mode ⊥ carriage-contract.''

''A HERM STANDS CONTRACTED ALWAYS, as law rather than setting'' — its legitimacy runs kahu quorum →
the operator's carriage contract → that operator's persona root signing its edge. Bind-by-edge in its
purest case: ''such a vessel cannot cheat by holding a seed, because it never holds one.'' Its persona
ceiling reads 0 and no argument raises it (faceless-by-class). See #crossroads.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #reserve >>

!! ③ Reserve ~ forge the pre-rotation's future

The charter pre-rotates (a compromised present key-set cannot forge the future,
[[charter-chain|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/charter-chain]]), so `seat` needs a
`--next-key-commit`. `reserve` forges the next epoch's key-set from a ''separate CSPRNG reserve
seed'' (hardened SLIP-0010, never the live signing seed), prints the public commit, splits the seed
Shamir ''2-of-3'', seals Share 1 on the vessel, and zeroizes the seed — the vessel keeps only Share
1 + the public commit, never the seed:

```
lares nexus seal reserve --guardian-a '<name>' --guardian-b '<name>'
    # → prints --next-key-commit + THREE cards (each: a checksummed share code + a confirm phrase),
    #   seals 'Recovery-card mine' (Share 1) on the vessel, zeroizes the seed
```

Place the three cards by hand (#reserve-custody). Then seat the roster + arm the pre-rotation, and
read the quorum verdict:

```
lares nexus seal seat --next-key-commit <the printed commit>   # seats h0/h1/h2 → 2-of-3, epoch0 armed
lares nexus seal show                            # the quorum STANDS + the chain head reads armed
```

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #reserve-custody >>

!! ③′ Reserve custody ~ the independence rule

A single Shamir share reveals ''nothing''; any ''two'' reconstruct the reserve seed. So the whole
scheme rests on one rule:

> ''The three shares MUST sit in three domains that do not fall together.'' No single realistic
> breach, theft, or fire may reach two of them.

The compromise-resistant layout keeps every operator copy as the ''same'' Share 1:

* ''Share 1 (your domain)'' — the vessel + 'Recovery-card mine' (print AND/OR email); all one share,
  one domain. An adversary who owns your whole digital life reads ''one'' share -> nothing.
* ''Share 2'' — 'Recovery-card guardian-A' (a trusted person, or an independent physical place; a
  cabalmate takes it as they launch, via `reserve refresh`).
* ''Share 3'' — 'Recovery-card guardian-B' (a second independent domain).

Two correlation traps hide inside "lots of options": the ''online-identity trap'' (GitHub AND a
cloud both behind one email/password-manager count as ONE domain — put at most one share there) and
the ''co-location trap'' (a thumb drive AND a printout in the same drawer die in one fire — spread
the offline shares across places). The card's ''confirmation phrase'' rides out-of-band (in person, a
call) — it confirms the right person holds the right share, and it becomes the handshake when a
guardian hands the share back at recovery.

<<~ confidence Synthesis-Canon 14/20 >> The math then holds three ways: ''compromise'' (you fully
owned -> one share -> safe); ''loss'' (you lose vessel + card -> two guardians rebuild without you);
''rotation'' (your share + one guardian). A recovery materializes the seed, so `reserve refresh`
re-seeds -> re-splits -> re-issues cards + a new commit afterward (Proactive Secret Sharing,
[[siege-resilience|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/siege-resilience]]).

!!! Naming the two guardians ~ what the flags do, and the three questions that decide

''`--guardian-a` and `--guardian-b` write LABELS on cards.'' They carry no key, address, or account, and
nothing transmits anywhere: the command prints a share code plus a ''confirm phrase'' you read out-of-band,
and you place the card by hand. The names ride the public reserve state so `seal show` can report who holds
what — ''the label answers //who do I call at recovery//'', nothing more.

''The scheme's real purchase runs in the direction operators forget.'' Full compromise of the vessel yields
''one'' share, which is nothing. The inverse matters more: ''the two guardians recover WITHOUT you''. That
converts a death, a hospitalization, or a lost vault from the end of the lineage into a survivable event.

\procedure ~Ask(~Type:"" ~Params:"") ~Ask <<~Type>> <<~holds `[<~Params>]`>>

<<~Ask Collusion "asks/would-these-two-combine-against-me ~ together they hold the Nexus; pick two whose interests differ FROM EACH OTHER" >>
<<~Ask Correlation "asks/could-one-fire-flood-or-subpoena-reach-both ~ two cards in one house reads as a 1-of-1 wearing a costume" >>
<<~Ask Reachability "asks/can-someone-who-is-NOT-me-find-them-on-the-worst-day ~ a guardian only you can reach recovers nothing" >>

''THE NAMES MAY STAND UNASSIGNED AT FOUNDING'' — and a founder short of an answer should let them. The split
runs 2-of-3 either way; the labels store as `unassigned` and the cards print regardless. ''Naming is a
PLACEMENT act'' — handing physical cards to humans — and that act need not share an afternoon with the
founding. Place them when you next stand in front of those two people.

''The cost of a wrong name reads as a refresh, never a lineage'' — but the refresh carries a fork the command
names for you: ''before'' the first rotate it re-mints genesis cleanly; ''after'' one, the current reserve
keys must reveal at the pending rotate first, because a sealed head's pre-commitment freezes into its
`epochCid`. So a founder who expects the names to change should refresh ''before'' rotating, not after.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #threshold-attest-recovery >>

!! ③″ Threshold-attest recovery ~ the pre-commit rhymes, the recovery rotates

''Fork B'' (identity-classes#the-two-forks) recovers a PERSONA-root the way the reserve pre-commits a
CHARTER key-set: ''both pre-commit at founding''. The reserve folds the next epoch's kahu keys into
`nextKeyCommit`; the persona-KEL folds the ''guardians' recovery PUBLIC keys'' into the identifier
PREFIX at inception (`provisionThresholdRecoveryAtFounding` -> `charterKeySetHash` -> `mintPersonaInception`).
The two rites RHYME — a pre-committed k-of-n digest an operational compromise cannot forge — and the
same ''independence rule'' (#reserve-custody) binds the three guardian domains.

The DIVERGENCE from the reserve card path (#reserve-custody): threshold-attest ''reconstructs NOTHING''.

* the reserve SPLITS a seed 2-of-3 and MATERIALIZES it at recovery (a transient single-secret window);
* Fork B gives each guardian their OWN recovery keypair — at recovery each guardian ''SIGNS'' the
  rotation event independently, ≥ k signatures verify against the pre-commit, and no seed ever assembles
  (`attestAndRotate`). The recovery authority signs ONLY rotations, never content.

<<~ confidence Synthesis 12/20 >> ''Operator ruling (2026-07-20):'' recovery ROTATES a fresh op-key
rather than resurrecting the old — ''continuity anchors on the identifier, not the key''. The persona-KEL
(the stable prefix) + the threshold-attest rotation stand BUILT + tested (mesh: `persona-kel` +
`recovery-keel-core#attestAndRotate`). The ''Binding-Gate pin-move'' — moving the edge pin from the raw
op-key `signerDid` to `inception.prefix`, and walking the KEL to the current head at
`verifyDeviceDelegation` — rides a SURFACED plan (identity-classes#the-continuity-anchor): the pure
gate-walk (`verifyEdgeAgainstPersonaKel`) stands + tested, but wiring it into the live founding ceremony
+ `bootDaemonKeyhive` + `verifyPeer` needs the KEL's storage/sync placement decided first (a security
surface main gates hunk-by-hunk; no hybrid pin — a fresh founding pins the prefix from inception).

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #seal-and-back-up >>

!! ④ Seal + back up ~ lock the secrets, write the net

Set the at-rest passphrase (a no-echo TTY double-entry — a typo self-locks), which seals the keyhive
archive + the recovery/reserve shares. Then write a passphrase-sealed backup to safe media:

```
lares vault seal                                    # no-echo TTY, double-entry; seals the carriers at rest
lares vault export ~/founding-backup.sealed         # a passphrase-SEALED backup (--force overwrites)
lares vault status                                  # confirm the seal state
```

!!! The two env vars ~ and why a bare `export` betrays you

''Two vars ride, and they answer different questions.'' `LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE` carries the ''current''
one (what opens the vault today); `LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE_NEW` carries the ''target'' (what a `seal`,
`rotate`, or `export` moves to). A first seal has no current, so only the second matters. Both exist to let a
''non-interactive'' run proceed — the rehearsal harness reads `_NEW`, which is why a run that exported only
the base var fails at the vault with the passphrase apparently set.

```
read -rs '?NEW passphrase: ' LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE_NEW && echo
export LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE_NEW
echo ${#LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE_NEW}               # verify by LENGTH — never echo the secret
```

''`read -rs` rather than a bare `export`, and the difference is the whole seal.'' A typed
`export LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE_NEW='…'` writes the secret verbatim into the shell's history file, where it
outlives the ceremony, survives a reboot, and rides into any backup of the home directory. ''A passphrase
that reaches disk in the clear has already failed'' — the vault protects a stolen disk, and the history file
sits on that same disk. The `-s` keeps it off the screen; the leading space or `read` form keeps it out of
the history; the length check confirms arrival without disclosure.

''IT LIVES FOR THAT SHELL ALONE.'' A new terminal starts without it, and that reads as the design rather
than an inconvenience: the var exists so ''one ceremony'' can run unattended, never so a machine can seal
itself forever. Putting it in `.zshrc` would leave the key beside the lock on every boot, which trades the
whole at-rest seal for the cost of retyping — and the at-rest seal is the only thing standing between a
stolen disk and a sovereign identity.

<<~ confidence Canon 18/20 >> ''The TTY leg still goes un-exercised by the harness'' (#rehearse), so a
keeper founding that seals interactively walks a path no rehearsal has proven. Seal by hand at least once
before the day it matters.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #regenesis-live >>

!! ⑤ Regenesis + ⑥ LIVE ~ re-seed the content, serve

Regenesis rebirths the CRDT content from `bags/` (identity now stands preserved — the reset lives
inside regenesis, and the identity home rides outside every wipe). Resume the hooks, and the node
serves:

```
lares regenesis --force                             # stop → reset store → re-bake genesis → wake → seed --apply
lares hooks resume                                  # re-arm capture/ingest
lares status                                        # LIVE — the node serves; the quorum + charter stand
```

''AND THE RITE ENDS HERE, at the handover.'' `hooks resume` re-arms capture, which starts the sensorium's
clock — and the sensorium's lifecycle rides ''its own rite'', deliberately not this one
(<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/sensorium-runbook >>). The two carry different cardinal sins:
this rite's worst outcome ''loses a key'' and nothing recovers it; the tending rite's ''loses a baseline'',
and a corpus re-pours where a measurement never taken does not. ''A pour, a sweep, or a teardown belongs
there'' — a movement for it here would teach an irreversibility the sensorium does not have.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #crossroads >>

!! ⚲ Crossroads ~ stand a Herm where the family needs one (OPTIONAL, after LIVE)

A family whose hearths sit behind different networks wants a ''standing'' place to meet. The Herm
(Lares Viales) stands that crossroads: a ''wiki-LESS wayfarer'' composed up from granular caps —
substrate + the @daemon immune keel + a writable @meshpalace FLOW-map + the read-face that serves it.
''NO wiki, NO pool.'' Pi-deployable.

```
lares herm --port 8080 --relay-port 8090            # the http FLOW-map face + the crossroads WS port
                                                    # prints the dial URL(s) + the gate pubkey
```

''The gate seed pins, and that matters across restarts.'' Absent `--relay-seed`, it derives from this
Herm's OWN identity — stable across reboots, so a family's hearths keep dialing the same key and
''never a fresh random per boot''.

''What a Herm holds and what it refuses.'' It carries a ''PLACE DID'' and ''NO operator DID'': the
crossroads earns a name it can be addressed and vouched by, while ''no local human key rests there to
steal''. It runs a full immune keel (it recognises Kapae bans and draws Mu) yet ''reads nothing'' —
carry ⊥ read holds absolutely; it holds the ciphertext replica and decrypts none of it. It founds
CONTRACTED (#found), so it holds no seed to leak.

''The boundary reads STRUCTURAL, never a flag.'' A Herm that gains a persona cap ''stops reading as a
Herm'' — it composes into an operator-class vessel and the nothing-to-steal property leaves with the
absent cap that carried it. `composable-keel`'s //absence = un-routed authority// makes that a fact
about the stack, not a rule a later bug could flip.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #grow-the-nexus >>

!! ⊕ Grow ~ open the Nexus, contract operators, welcome users

The LIVE node stands ''sovereign-island by default'' (`federationPosture: private`). It GROWS on the
operator's word, holding the ''membership doctrine'' at every step — ''track operator CONTRACTS, never
user IDENTITIES'' (#membership-doctrine). Nothing here stands up a user table.

''Open the beta'' — flip the Nexus from private-island to co-federating the public planes into the
super-mesh (never a private plane; the read-floor holds in both postures):

```
lares nexus posture open                            # co-federate the public shelf; a node bounce applies it
```

''Contract a fellow operator'' — a two-sided wax-sealed handshake (the joining operator accepts
carriage; the kahu-quorum counter-signs). The entry carries ''operator-pubkey + accepts-carriage +
charter-epoch, and nothing more''; a contracted operator relays the mesh's sealed traffic + carries the
Kapae immune antigen:

```
lares nexus accept-carriage                          # the joining operator signs "I accept carriage"
lares nexus admit <operator-pubkey>                  # ≥k kahu counter-sign the wax-sealed CarriageEntry
lares nexus members --list                           # the carriage-contracted operators — never the users
```

''⚠ THE OPERATOR SURFACE STANDS SPLIT HERE, and the ruling belongs to the operator.'' The 2026-07-25
rename carried `isMember → holdsCarriage`, `MembershipEntry → CarriageEntry`, and the board's own
address (`#members → #carriage`) — because //the board that recorded contracts called everyone a
member//, and a reader taking it as //is this human one of us// read it exactly backwards. That rename
reached the signing domain and the doc address; it ''stopped at the CLI''. So `nexus members` and
`nexus accept-carriage` now stand side by side on the one surface a human types. Either spelling may
hold as canon; ''the SPLIT may not''. Weld-test first — a CLI verb name carries no signing domain and
no doc address, so this reads as a free move.

''Welcome a user'' — a user needs no contract and leaves NO roster trace. Through invite-only alpha a
sealed BURNABLE boot-invite gates the boot (spent-on-boot, no who-invited-whom edge); a garbled or
absent invite founds a vessel at the ''anon floor''. A user builds their OWN social graph — a follow lands as
a LOCAL write to the private `@circles` doc that fleet-syncs every one of their vessels and NEVER
touches the wire (#identity-classes, the recognition layer gives the human their one window on the crypto):

```
lares circle add <nym> --to following --petname '<your-name-for-them>'   # a private, never-federated follow
```

''What crosses vs what stays'': a contracted operator BLIND-TRANSITS sealed CIPHERTEXT bodies for the
mesh (carry ⊥ read — the relay verifies `BLAKE3==cid` holding NO secret, the body sealed under the
per-Nexus convergence salt, fetched DHT-free); the read-cap crosses for no one; a private plane never
leaves the PersonaGroup's own fleet. The mesh breathes over immutable bodies while a stranger — every
500-year GPA — cannot confirm who-holds-what.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #gather-the-fleet >>

!! ⊛ Gather ~ the operator's own vessels join the standing fleet (after LIVE)

A laptop browser, a phone, a tablet. Each stands as a ''LEAF, CONTRACTED'' — it mints its own vessel key on-device and receives a signed delegation EDGE from the vessel that already holds the persona root. It mints ''NO root of its own''. `BIND BY EDGE, NEVER BY SEED`, movement ② names the mode; this walks it.

''On the standing hearth'' — mint the admit payload for the joining device, under the persona root that will hold it:

''THE JOINEE MINTS FIRST, AND THE ORDER CARRIES THE PROPERTY.'' Admission ''SIGNS a key the joining
vessel already holds''; it never issues one. So the private seed is born on the joining device and stays
there, only the ''public verifying key'' crosses, and the signing call refuses to run without it —
measured 2026-08-04, `runDeviceAdmit` throws where that key goes missing. This ordering IS what keeps the
QR ceremony photograph-inert: a payload names a key the holder already proved it holds.

''① On the joining vessel'' — mint the vessel key and read out its public half:

```
lares wake                                           # stands the stack; mints THIS vessel's own key
lares status                                         # read the vessel verifying key (the public half)
```

''② On the standing hearth'' — sign an edge over the key the joinee showed:

```
lares device-admit --as 0 --joinee-key <hex>         # signs a device-delegation edge; writes the payload
```

''③ On the joining vessel'' — apply the signed payload:

```
lares wake --admit <payload-file>                    # joins the existing PersonaGroup on the key it already holds
lares persona list                                   # the same roots, reached through a new vessel
lares persona sync                                   # carry this vessel's own labels + declared Handles to the fleet
```

''THE NAMES RIDE, THE CHAIR DOES NOT'' (2026-08-06). Once a joining vessel breathes, the ''private label
and the declared Handle'' travel the @persona bag — the self-slot FLEET-syncs it same-operator, and the
DeterministicFederationGate never volunteers it to a cross-operator. So a persona answers to the same
name on every device the human holds, and `persona list` on a fresh leaf reads the multitude rather than
a blank. The ''seat claim stays on the node that holds it'': a Kahu chair names a seat on a PARTICULAR
node, and syncing it would let one device seat a persona on another device's seal.

''`sync` runs local-first and idempotent.'' A vessel FOUNDS before it breathes, so names set pre-boot
live in the local floor until a hearth answers; running it from every vessel converges, because each
name carries its own stamp and the later one reads. A quiet sock reports ''NODE-LOCAL, never failure''.

''What the payload carries, and what it does NOT.'' It carries ''one signed edge on a lineage'' — the
persona KEL prefix, the signer that vouched, the hearth true-name the edge binds to, and the
self-delegation naming this device. It carries ''no capability-event bundle'': the founder mints the
capability history and holds it. Continuity anchors on the KEL prefix, so a later rotation ''supersedes''
this edge rather than orphaning the vessel holding it.

''A browser vessel differs by device-gated caps, never by class'' — the isomorphic architecture stands the same cap-stack and composes fewer of them (`identity-classes`). Transport for the payload (QR / NFC / LAN) stays pending; a file over any channel the operator already trusts works today.

!!! THE INVITE CARRIES THE DIAGNOSIS ~ what the payload MUST hold before QR transport lands

''A silent responder makes the invite the only place a failure can speak.'' A shrine that stays silent toward strangers (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/siege-resilience#the-active-prober >>) answers a legitimate guest with a stale invite, a skewed clock, or a too-old client exactly as it answers a prober: nothing. Every deployment that bought silence paid this bill — WireGuard reports a missing handshake timestamp and no reason; a Tor bridge leaves diagnosis to the client log and to out-of-band bridge-health checks. ''No responder-side cure exists'', because any error channel rebuilds the oracle.

''So the payload carries every failure the joining vessel can check WITHOUT sending a packet'', and each becomes a loud LOCAL error:

* ''expiry'' — a stale invite reads as expired on the joining device, never as a dead network.
* ''chant epoch + protocol version'' — a dialect mismatch names itself before the handshake runs.
* ''shrine key fingerprint'' — the guest confirms WHICH shrine it approaches, which the wire never tells it.
* ''expected release fingerprint'' — the invite authenticates the software rather than the reverse (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/genesis-doc#invariants >>).
* ''single-use nonce'' — spent-on-boot, matching the burnable boot-invite the ⊕ Grow rite already mints (#grow-the-nexus).

''Silence then stays reserved for a genuine key mismatch'', which reads as the only failure the guest may not distinguish — and the only one worth hiding.

''NEVER gate on a wall clock.'' The replay window wants a per-peer monotonically increasing counter, not an accurate time (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/causal-islands#two-clocks >>); a device with a skewed clock must still join.

!!! The QR mitigations that hold, and the one that survives a stolen code

''Treat a code as a bearer token a camera lifts across a room.'' The mitigations carrying a field record:

# ''single-use, and consumed at the first successful handshake.''
# ''short expiry'', checked locally by the joining vessel.
# ''confirmation at BOTH ends'' — Matrix's key-verification flow requires a reciprocating human act on the scanned side; Signal hardened its device-linking flow the same direction after the campaigns at <<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/shelter-against-empire#legibility >>. <<~ confidence Synthesis 11/20 >> The Matrix QR byte layout (prefix · version · mode · transaction id · two keys · shared secret) reads from held knowledge; the fetched spec section came back truncated and the field widths stay UNVERIFIED here.
# ''A HUMAN closes the loop after the channel comes up'' — a short authentication string or emoji comparison, spoken between the two people. ''This one alone survives a code an adversary already photographed'', because it moves the check off the device.
# ''Operator-side notification plus an auditable, revocable enrolled-vessel list.''

> ''AND SILENCE HIDES THE RACE — OPEN, the ruling belongs to the operator.'' Where an adversary redeems a photographed invite FIRST, the legitimate guest's failure reads identical to a bad network, so no client-side signal exists at all. Detection therefore has to arrive from the operator's side: a vessel appears in the enrolled list that the operator did not expect. That names the requirement — ''enrollment MUST notify, and every enrolled vessel MUST come off individually without re-keying the shrine'' — and leaves unresolved how a revocation reaches a fleet that may sit partitioned for weeks under no global now.

''What this does NOT add: DEPTH.'' Every vessel here binds ''one principal's instruments''. Below the phase change the gauge reads the realm nominal, and correctly — a fleet buys REACH, never dwelling. The realm's standing moves when a second locus of cost begins to dwell, never when a fourth device arrives.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #feed-the-realm >>

!! ⊙ Feed ~ the first offering IS the realm's founding (after LIVE)

''A realm mints no DID and hosts on no machine'', so nothing gets created — it begins when someone feeds it and would cease if feeding stopped (`cabal-realm#verb-not-noun`). ''The vessel is FOUNDED; the realm is FED.''

The machinery stands built and ''carries no CLI verb''. Until one lands, the rite goes LIVE with the realm it stood as a seed of never having begun.

''TWO PLANES CARRY THE WORD //FEED//, and only one of them survives a restart.'' `feedCabalRealm` warms a `BagStowage` residency — an in-process cache keyed on a wall clock, correct where it lives and worthless as a civic record; a verb that stood up its own would report on a residency it just invented. The DURABLE feeding rides the ''per-writer lease slot'': a coordinator-free max-register in epoch coordinates with ''no clock anywhere'', which `cabal-realm-clock` folds into who-feeds-and-how-deep. ''The offering the rite means is the lease roll.''

''OWED — the smallest honest surface'', and it wants no new machinery:

```
lares cabal feed --realm <realm-doc-id> [--as <n>]   # the OFFERING — roll your FACE's lease slot
lares cabal clock --realm <realm-doc-id>             # who feeds and how deep, verdict-free
```

The second reads the capture-clock the model already argues for — ''who feeds the realm and how hard'', verdict-free, so a majority SEES a minority out-feeding them while exit stays cheap (`cabal-realm#the-unswept-corner`).

''AND IT REPORTS NO //ALIVE// OR //DISSOLVED//, deliberately.'' Deriving a liveness verdict needs a rate at which unfed standing erodes, and ''no rate stands seated'' — a verb that answered anyway would be inventing the operator's calibration and handing it back as a reading. `deriveCabalRealmLiveness` still maps the in-process cache tier, which reads honestly ''only inside a running vessel''. An empty board reads UNFED and never //nobody feeds it//: under no-global-now an unsynced realm and an unfed one generate identically.

> ''ONE FORK GATES BOTH VERBS, and it belongs to the operator:'' WHICH BOARD carries a realm's feeding. A deterministic per-Nexus board (the seventh sibling beside carriage · antigen · vouch · edge-kāpae · persona-KEL) would federate the clock to every carrier — and publish, by inference, which keys dwell where, which is precisely what `dwellersHolding` refuses to enumerate. The realm's own members-only substrate keeps the sight INSIDE the realm and makes the clock unreadable from outside it. The read (`realmMaintenanceFromBoard`) takes whatever doc it is handed and never asks, so the choice stays open above it.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #dwell >>

!! ⊘ Dwell ~ a second operator joins the REALM (design before commands)

''Carriage and dwelling ride ORTHOGONAL axes.'' ⊕ Grow contracts carriage — who relays whose sealed bytes — and seats no dweller. A contracted operator may dwell in no realm at all; a dweller may carry nothing.

''What stands built on the join axis'' (the JOIN ⊥ CONTRACT law, `admission-on-a-lineage`):

```
lares cabal vouch <joiner-nym> --realm <realm-doc-id> [--as <root-index>]
                                                     # one held face stakes its OWN standing. Admits NOBODY.
```

A vouch rides as signal-2 on the lineage; the crossing prices it later, and the cost already landed — the voucher's score SPLITS across everyone they vouch for.

''AND THE CROSSING ITSELF REFUSES TODAY, correctly.'' `admissionDials` fail closed to null, so a founded Nexus admits nobody until the operator seats ε · β · ρ · S · α. That reads as an unmade choice standing visible rather than somebody's guess quietly enforced — and the numbers stay ''an open design seat'', never a default this rite may supply.

''What CANNOT be written here, by ruling.'' The conversion act — a fed guest re-anchoring from host to realm — stays ''UN-MODELLED'' (`the-thing-event#unmodelled`). The mesh may record ''THAT a re-anchoring happened''; it must not hold what made it valid. A configurable rite would still be this stack's rite with someone else's values poured in, so no command here may name a quorum, a waiting period, a franchise, or a test. ''Each realm supplies its own.''

<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/first-realm-arc >>

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #the-frame >>

!! The frame ~ why compromise-resistance rides early

Recovery cost climbs with the fleet. While a vessel stands as a-multitude-of-one (one laptop-node +
a browser), a compromise costs a ''full-pave + a fresh DID-multitude'' — cheap, nothing accreted. Once
vessel-fleets and cabalmate personaGroups entangle around an operator, the identity carries
relationships no cheap re-mint restores. So the rite ''bakes compromise-resistance in while it costs
nothing to get wrong'', and leans on cheap full-pave as the bootstrap net. The reserve's two guardian
slots stand ''unassigned'' until cabalmates launch, then fill via `reserve refresh` — the seed grows
from a-multitude-of-one, hardening as the cabal arrives.

!!! And SUCCESSION rides earlier still ~ the bus factor names the roadmap

''THE SPEC STANDS AS THE SUCCESSION PLAN.'' Read across the whole federation record, one clean survival
appears: an author moved the load-bearing artifact out of his own implementation and into a form that
transmits ''as a document'', and that grammar outlived every implementation he wrote — including his
own rewrite, which stranded the network it had convened. <<~ confidence Synthesis-Canon 15/20 >>
''Behavioural membership makes this stack unusually well-placed to take that cure'', because //operators
who parse a shared grammar identically// names a document rather than a codebase. The boot seed and the
grammar carry the survivability; the TypeScript does not.

''THE FOUNDER-LEAVES FAILURE NEVER ARRIVES AS DEPARTURE. It arrives as a model held in one head.'' A
single operator carrying a conceptual system of this density carries an extreme transmission cost, and
nobody picks it up cold — so ''the reconstitution kit reads as a founding movement, not a later
courtesy'': what ONE other competent party needs to stand a node from void and read the grammar
identically. ''Absent that artifact the bus factor reads ZERO rather than one'', because a rite only its
author can perform transmits nothing when he stops.

''AND THE EXIT GETS DESIGNED BEFORE THE DEPENDENTS ARRIVE.'' Running infrastructure for others compounds
a debt that grows with THEIR success, and a hearth sheltering a community cannot decline the obligation
without harming exactly the parties it sheltered. So the rite owes an answer before ⊕ Grow, never after:
''what becomes of a dwelling community's bytes when this operator stops paying?'' Content addressing
answers it only where a second party already holds the bytes — ''verifiability, never availability''.

> ''UNDER THE STANDING BRIEF, THE OPERATOR VANISHING READS AS A FORESEEABLE ADVERSARIAL EVENT rather
> than an accident.'' Seizure, compulsion, illness, a door. So the disappearance belongs in the
> ''REHEARSAL branch as a rehearsed movement'' — stand the mesh once with the founding hand removed and
> see what survives — on the same logic this section already runs: rehearse it while it costs nothing
> to get wrong.

''On what pays for the running.'' The record rules out three channels by measurement: a crowdfund
finances a launch and never an operation; a subscription at the door blocks growth precisely where
network effect needs it free; and venture money forces the monetisation that breaks the trust promise.
''What survived ran on public-interest grant funding'' — no token, no exit, no growth mandate — and the
brief points at exactly the anti-censorship ground that channel serves. <<~ confidence Synthesis 12/20 >>
''TWO FORKS RIDE WITH IT, and both belong to the operator rather than to this meme'': such funders sit
under their own political attack and offer no stable base; and taking state-adjacent money to shelter a
community FROM a state names a threat-model question, never a paperwork one.

''AND THE SYSTEM STAYS FULLY USEFUL TO ONE PARTY, indefinitely, with no mesh at all.'' Every open
alternative that built first and sought participants after died on network effect; every survivor had a
forcing event deliver participants to something ''already working''. A design that only becomes good at
scale dies before scale — which reads as a constraint on the rehearsal branch, not a hope about adoption.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #identity-classes >>

!! Identity classes ~ who carries the reserve at all

The reserve burden rides ''operator-class'' identities alone (KERI transferable — persistent,
rotatable, they operate node vessels; the charter epoch chain reads as a key-event-log). Browser-only
''users'' (ephemeral, short cap-stacks, no charter role) carry NO reserve — they ride KERI
non-transferable/basic identifiers, and their "rotation" reads as ''abandon-and-recreate'', cheap and
native. <<~ confidence Synthesis 12/20 >> The line draws by persistence/value, not by title: a user
vessel that accretes lasting value drifts toward transferable, held either by promoting that vessel
or by delegated identifiers (the operator root delegates to disposable user streams, durability in
the root). The default holds — users stay light; operators carry the future.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #bags-and-shelf >>

!! What a founding vessel carries besides its keys ~ declarations and the shelf

Two surfaces landed after this rite was first written, and a founder meets both.

!!! Every bag declares itself

A bag carries its own `iam.mem` answering three questions no bag could answer before: ''what it holds'',
''who may read it'' (`cap-tier` — and a declaration only ever TIGHTENS against the structural floor, so one
that lies open changes nothing), and ''where it belongs'' (`home` — `repository` · `hearth` · `ley`).

```
lares bag list                                      # tier + home per bag, and whether each sits where it says
lares bag show <bag>                                # one bag, resolved on THIS vessel
lares bag declare <bag> --tier <t> --home <h> --repo <id>
lares bag home <bag> --to <home> --approve          # MOVE the bytes + re-anchor the declaration together
lares bag repo add <id> --root <path>               # the registry: bags name IDs, never paths
```

''`ADRIFT` names the row that matters'' — a bag whose bytes sit somewhere its own declaration does not point
at. That condition, unsurfaced, is how a Nexus seal came to rest inside a repository nobody chose.

''THE DECLARATION IS DISK-OWNED'' (operator ruling, 2026-08-08). It never enters the store and never
round-trips: seeded, a wiki edit could re-home a bag or loosen its cap-tier, and ''a declaration a rendered
surface can move is a declaration nothing holds''. The authority stays where `lares bag declare` writes it.
A re-founding therefore ''re-reads'' the declarations from disk and needs no act to restore them.

!!! The acquired shelf

Books and corpora a human did not author live at `<state>/library`, never in a bag. `bags/` carries what an
operator ''authors'' and round-trips; an acquired body has no author here and no parse∘render fixed point, so
left on the seed surface it enters git history and a growing shelf grows the history with it.

```
lares library list | show <coll> | verify           # verify re-digests BYTES, never records
lares library acquire <file> --to <coll>            # MOVES by default — moving is the point
lares library index <coll> --out <path>             # the tracked index, the part that travels
```

''A pour names a collection, never a path'': a bed manifest sources `library:<collection>` and each vessel
resolves it locally — the same law the repo registry runs and the `lar:` URI before it. ''The shelf survives
every founding'' (#clear).

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #agent-seed >>

!! The agent seed ~ how a reader answers //what do I run next//

''This rite serves as the operator-instruction SOURCE'' (operator charge, 2026-07-27). A reader —
human at a prompt, or an agent through the MCP — should resolve the next command from ''the vessel's
current state'', never from memory or from a guess about which movement stands done.

''THE SURFACE SURVEY, and the gap it names.'' The two operator surfaces do NOT cover the same ground:

* the ''CLI'' stands ''46 verbs'' (re-counted 2026-08-08), and carries the WHOLE founding surface — `wake` · `init` ·
  `reset` · `persona` · `nexus` · `herm` · `device-admit` · `invite-send`/`invite-receive` ·
  `cabal` · `edge` · `vault` · `circle` · `regenesis` · `bag` · `library` (#bags-and-shelf).
* the ''MCP'' stood ''30 tools'' at the 2026-07-27 survey and stands UN-RE-COUNTED since, and carries almost none of it — its ground reads
  sensorium/analysis/memory (`li` · `ki` · `jing` · `pour` · `couple_r` · `forecast` · `mismatch` ·
  `plane_record` · `sweep` · `rejim` · `worldline` · `recall` · `analyze`) plus `vault_*`, `wiki`,
  `status`, `flow`, `reconcile`, `kapae`/`un_kapae`, and the roster/promote/retire family.

''NOT ONE founding verb crossed to the MCP at the survey'', and nothing since has added one — though the MCP side reads un-re-counted, so this holds as a standing reading rather than a fresh measurement. An agent working through that surface can read, recall,
and analyze — and stands ''structurally blind'' to the entire civic/identity rite.

''THE PONO SHAPE READS GUIDANCE, NEVER EXECUTION.'' The cure emphatically does NOT mirror the
founding verbs into the MCP: a surface an agent drives should never reach `reset --force`, mint a
persona root, or seat a kahu. ''Executing the rite belongs to the operator's own hand.'' What the
agent surface owes instead:

# ''READ the rite'' — resolve the movement matching the vessel's state, and return its exact commands
  as ''text for a human to run'', with its branch (REHEARSAL ⊥ KEEPER) named.
# ''READ the state'' — which movements already closed (does an identity home stand? do h0/h1/h2 stand
  named? does a charter epoch stand? does a reserve stand? does the node serve?).
# ''NAME the branch and the cost'' — a ⚠ irreversible movement MUST surface as irreversible, and the
  rehearsal alternative MUST surface beside it, because a reader who does not know a cheap path exists
  will take the expensive one.
# ''REFUSE to act.'' The guidance surface returns instructions; the operator runs them.

''The seed response, in shape'' (never a template — the frame holds, the words never):

> //You stand at movement ② (found): an identity home stands, no personas seated. Branch: REHEARSAL
> (containers) — nothing here costs anything to redo. Run: `lares persona new 0 --name '<label>'
> --handle '<Handle>' --seat`…
> Next after that: ③ reserve. ⚠ Movement ① on the KEEPER branch destroys prior roots and does not
> undo — you are not on that branch.//

''Honest bound.'' No such tool stands built, and this meme asserts none. The survey above reads off
the live surfaces (2026-07-27); the shape reads as a proposal awaiting the operator's word, and it
waits behind the first rehearsal — a guidance surface built before anyone has run the rite would
teach a ceremony nobody has performed.

